  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NESA Center Executive Seminar 01-20: &quot;Great Power, Strategic Rivalries, and Proxy Wars&quot;</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">These flagship courses seek to stimulate dialogue on strategic issues affecting the Near East South Asia region among national security professionals. Topics include U.S. foreign policy; the current and future regional strategic issues; counterterrorism; the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction, and disaster management. The theme for this Executive Seminar 01-20 is: &quot;Great Power, Strategic Rivalries, and Proxy Wars.&quot; This seminar will address the reality of the global power dynamics in the world. Once thought of as a relic of the 20th century, with larger countries able to project power in different parts of the world are doing so, whether in the form of fueling conflicts, enacting sanctions or other measures. Whether these are for specific national security reasons or to limit the influence of other states remains a point of debate. In the NESA AOR, these rivalries are particularly problematic, as they are often superimposed upon regional rivalries and dynamics that are just as difficult. Examples can be found in North Africa, the Middle East, South and Central Asia, underscoring the need for NESA region countries to better understand the impact and cost of the strategic rivalries between the United States, Russia and China, as well as the regional parallels that exist.</narrative>
  </description>
